Z||000002||Cozmo||05-25-2001||07:38 AM||||Must be they're all gettin' around 100K miles on 'em finally... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Just did mine, weekend project $60 worth of parts.  Tedious but if the rest of the engine is OK not too bad of a job.  The old stripped gear is now hanging in the shop on the "Wall of Shame" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR><B>C02M0</B>||||reg||10
Z||000003||Fformula88||05-25-2001||04:32 PM||||Depends on who does the work.  If your doing it yourslef, try replacing the gear.  However, if your having the work done its probably more cost effective to replace the motor.  My father had the timing gears replaced in his 87 coupe... cost about $1000!  A month later an o-ring went causing massive blow by.  Another $2200 went for a new engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||05-25-2001||11:15 PM||||IF you do replace the gear I would strongly suggest that you use the metal timing gear.  Only costs about $25 more and should last the life of the engine.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000005||86sc||05-26-2001||12:25 AM||||ill going to go with the metal gears, if i rebuild it. i haven't decided on rebuld or replacement just yet.<BR> ||||reg||6
Z||000006||Bob Englert||05-26-2001||10:31 AM||||Even if you are just replacing the gear, go with the metal one.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
